  "tutorialOfferTitle": "New to Quadrim?",
  "tutorialOfferBody": "A short tutorial explains the cube and how a game is won. It takes a couple of minutes, and you can leave at any point.",
  "@tutorialOfferBody": {
    "description": "Shown once, on first launch. Says the tutorial can be left so the offer does not read as a commitment."
  },
  "tutorialOfferAccept": "Show me",
  "tutorialOfferDecline": "Not now",
  "tutorialGoalTitle": "The goal",
  "tutorialGoalBody": "Put four of your pieces in a straight line and you win. Lines run in every direction through the cube, and there are 76 of them.",
  "tutorialGoalBoard": "Layer 1, row 1: four light pieces filling the row, highlighted as a completed line.",
  "@tutorialGoalBoard": {
    "description": "Screen-reader description of one tutorial board. The board itself is silenced and described as a whole, because swiping through 64 cells to find four highlighted ones is not comprehension (D195)."
  },
  "tutorialLayersTitle": "Four layers, one cube",
  "tutorialLayersBody": "The board is a cube: four squares wide, four deep, four tall. It is drawn as four layers, one under the other, with layer 1 at the front and layer 4 at the back. The same row and column in all four layers is one straight line through the cube.",
  "tutorialLayersBoard": "Row 2, column 2 of every layer, highlighted: one line running from the front of the cube to the back.",
  "tutorialPlacingTitle": "Taking a turn",
  "tutorialPlacingBody": "Tap any empty square to place a piece. Light goes first, then the players alternate. A piece never moves once it is placed, and the game ends the moment somebody makes a line.",
  "tutorialPlacingBoard": "Layer 2 holds two light pieces and one dark piece, layer 3 holds one dark piece, and every other square is empty.",
  "tutorialLayerDiagonalTitle": "Diagonals inside a layer",
  "tutorialLayerDiagonalBody": "A line can run corner to corner across a single layer. Both diagonals of each of the four layers count.",
  "tutorialLayerDiagonalBoard": "Layer 2, highlighted corner to corner: row 1 column 1, row 2 column 2, row 3 column 3, row 4 column 4.",
  "tutorialSliceDiagonalTitle": "Diagonals through the layers",
  "tutorialSliceDiagonalBody": "Take a slice through the cube — one row, all four layers deep — and it has diagonals of its own. These are the lines new players miss.",
  "tutorialSliceDiagonalBoard": "Row 1 of every layer, highlighted: column 1 in layer 1, column 2 in layer 2, column 3 in layer 3, column 4 in layer 4.",
  "tutorialCubeDiagonalTitle": "Corner to corner",
  "tutorialCubeDiagonalBody": "Four lines run right through the middle of the cube, from one corner to the opposite one. They are the hardest to see and the easiest to lose to.",
  "tutorialCubeDiagonalBoard": "Highlighted from row 1 column 1 of layer 1 to row 4 column 4 of layer 4, moving one row and one column with every layer.",
  "tutorialRulesDoneTitle": "That is the whole game",
  "tutorialRulesDoneBody": "Rows, columns, the lines through the layers, and every diagonal — 76 in all. That is everything you need to start playing. What follows is how games are actually won, which you can leave for later.",
  "tutorialFinishHere": "Finish here",
  "@tutorialFinishHere": {
    "description": "Ends the tutorial at the boundary between the rules and the strategy. Counts as completing it: a player who only wanted the rules has finished what they came for (ticket 019)."
  },
  "tutorialCentreTitle": "The middle is worth most",
  "tutorialCentreBody": "Squares are not equal. The eight in the middle of the cube — rows and columns 2 and 3, in layers 2 and 3 — each sit on seven different lines. Most squares sit on four. Taking the middle early gives you more ways to win than your opponent has.",
  "tutorialCentreBoard": "Eight squares highlighted on an empty board: rows 2 and 3, columns 2 and 3, in layers 2 and 3.",
  "tutorialCornersTitle": "Then the corners",
  "tutorialCornersBody": "The eight corners of the cube also sit on seven lines each — they are the only other squares that do. That is why an opponent that seems to be playing for nothing in particular is often quietly collecting corners.",
  "tutorialCornersBoard": "Eight squares highlighted on an empty board: the corner of each layer, at rows 1 and 4, columns 1 and 4, in layers 1 and 4.",
  "tutorialDoubleThreatTitle": "Two threats at once",
  "tutorialDoubleThreatBody": "One line needing one more piece can be blocked. Two of them cannot. Light has just played the corner, and it completed three of a row and three of a column in the same move. Both highlighted squares now win, and dark has one turn.",
  "tutorialDoubleThreatBoard": "Light has just played layer 1, row 1, column 1. Two squares are highlighted: layer 1 row 1 column 4, and layer 1 row 4 column 1. Either one wins for light, and dark can block only one of them.",
  "tutorialBlockingTitle": "Blocking before it is too late",
  "tutorialBlockingBody": "The same thing works against you. Dark holds three of a row in layer 2 and needs one more square. Light has to take the highlighted square now — and watch for the move that would give dark two threats, because by then it is already too late.",
  "tutorialBlockingBoard": "Dark holds layer 2, row 1, columns 1 to 3. The highlighted square is layer 2, row 1, column 4, which light must take.",
  "tutorialStrategyDoneTitle": "That is how they play",
  "tutorialStrategyDoneBody": "Take a win, block a loss, build two threats, and stop your opponent building them — over squares that sit on the most lines. That is not a simplification for the tutorial: it is what the computer opponents are doing, from the weakest up. The stronger ones just see further ahead. Try it out below, against one of the gentler ones.",
